Prompt: Write a single self-contained Python 3 command-line script that strips
content from Python source files, such as comments, docstrings, and other
removable elements, while preserving valid syntax and executable behavior.
The script must be runnable from the command line and should be intended for
execution under a Termux (Android) Python environment, so use a portable shebang
and avoid desktop-only assumptions.
Purpose and behavior: - Accept one or more input paths (files and/or
directories).
Directories should be traversed to find `.py` files.
Support an optional output destination or an in-place mode, and clearly report
which mode is active. - Provide a configuration object implemented as an
immutable, slotted, frozen dataclass named StripOptions with fields including a
backend selector (default "libcst"), a boolean remove_comments (default False),
an inline_comments flag, and other boolean options controlling what is stripped
(for example docstrings and blank lines, where applicable).
Define sensible defaults for every field. - Support two parsing/transformation
backends: libcst (preferred, round-trip preserving) and tree-sitter (via the
tree_sitter and tree_sitter_python packages).
Import each optionally inside try/except ImportError and record availability in
module-level boolean flags.
If the requested backend is unavailable, fail with a clear error message rather
than crashing with a traceback; fall back only if that is clearly documented. -
Use the standard library for argument parsing (argparse), file handling
(pathlib, tempfile for atomic writes), and parallel processing (multiprocessing)
for handling multiple files.
Use `from __future__ import annotations` and type hints throughout. - Configure
logging with loguru: remove the default handler, add a stderr sink with a
colored format showing timestamp, level, and message, at INFO level. - Use `ast`
to verify that the stripped output is still syntactically valid Python (parse
the output and compare or check it).
If verification fails, do not overwrite the original file; log an error and skip
or report that file. - Write output atomically: write to a temporary file in the
same directory and then replace the target, so partial writes never corrupt
sources. - Preserve file encoding (default UTF-8, respect PEP 263 coding
declarations where feasible) and original newline style where practical. -
Handle errors robustly: missing paths, unreadable or unwritable files, decode
errors, syntax errors in input, and backend exceptions should each be caught,
logged with the file path, and should not abort processing of the remaining
files.
Return a non-zero exit code if any file failed, and zero otherwise. - Handle
edge cases: empty files, files with only comments, files with a shebang line or
encoding cookie (keep these when comments are removed), non-Python files passed
explicitly (skip with a warning), symlinks (define and document a policy), and
paths containing spaces or Unicode. - Provide a dry-run option that reports what
would change without writing files, and a summary at the end showing counts of
processed, changed, skipped, and failed files. - Provide a worker-count option
controlling multiprocessing pool size, defaulting to the CPU count, and fall
back to sequential processing when only one file is given or when the count is
1.
Guard the main entry point with `if __name__ == "__main__":` and make
multiprocessing safe for spawn-based start methods. - Exit with a clean usage
message on invalid arguments.
Output requirements: produce the complete script in one code block with brief
docstrings and comments, and no external files beyond the listed dependencies
(loguru, libcst, tree-sitter, tree-sitter-python, all optional except loguru).
